734375, 0.0232391357421875, 0.032379150390625, -0.016510009765625, -0.0213470458984375, -0.0010404586791992188, 0.029998779296875, 0.01080322265625, -0.00960540771484375, 0.05438232421875, -0.034210205078125, 0.0091552734375, 0.01019287109375, 0.0210723876953125, 0.02996826171875, 0.0219268798828125, 0.0184173583984375, -0.0273590087890625, 0.03875732421875, -0.0002777576446533203, 0.01641845703125, -0.0003676414489746094, 0.0140533447265625, -0.00055694580078125, -0.01448822021484375, 0.00637054443359375, 0.01329803466796875, -0.0144195556640625, 0.0096435546875, 0.041168212890625, -0.0030651092529296875, -0.0159912109375, -0.0298614501953125, 0.007389068603515625, -0.01450347900390625, -0.003536224365234375, 0.0382080078125, 0.0072784423828125, 0.007396697998046875, -0.0214996337890625, -0.014190673828125, 0.005550384521484375, -0.01727294921875, -0.034271240234375, 0.00830841064453125, -0.029998779296875, 0.0019245147705078125, -0.01180267333984375, -0.005924224853515625, 0.0187530517578125, -0.000370025634765625, -0.00325775146484375, -0.0173492431640625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced due to improvements in the scale, quality, rebalancing, and enhanced recaptioning of multimodal datasets. However, most existing pipelines\u2014such as those underlying LAION-5B (Schuhmann et al., 2022)\u2014optimize individual, task-specific datasets in isolation. This practice restricts systematic transfer of supervision across related generative tasks, hinders efficient organization of multimodal data, and complicates unified progress across capabilities. A key unresolved challenge is how to curate and organize datasets to enable cross-capability transfer while faithfully capturing dependencies among core generative skills.\n\n---\n\n**Background, Definitions, and Motivation:**  \nOur approach is motivated by the need to unify and systematically structure multimodal data for advanced generative modeling. Here, a **capability** refers to a distinct, functional skill a generative model acquires (e.g., pairing text and images, performing image edits, or linking images to knowledge entities). An **engine** denotes a specific annotation or data collection pipeline focused on producing supervision signals for a single capability. A **scheduler** is a system that sequences the training or curation order based on empirically determined dependencies between capabilities.\n\nRecent progress in multimodal generation has depended on web-scale datasets and annotation pipelines. Yet, current practices treat critical tasks\u2014text-to-image synthesis, image editing, and entity association\u2014almost independently, missing opportunities to share supervision and training efficiency.\n\n---\n\n**Summary of Dataset and Contribution Overview:**  \nTable A summarizes our dataset composition and key statistics (detailed protocols follow in later sections):\n\n| Capability                     | Example                   | Instances (M) | % of Total | Key Subgroup Coverage      |\n|------------------------------- |--------------------------|---------------|------------|----------------------------|\n| Text-image grounding           | \"a red fire truck\"        | 440           | 75.0%      | FairFace, gender, objects  |\n| Inter-image transformation     | \"day to night edit\"       | 120           | 20.5%      | Age, weather, context      |\n| Image-knowledge association    | cityscape \u2192 Q2364         | 27            | 4.5%       | Geographic, rare entities  |\n\nAfter filtering, approximately 13% of data is discarded due to inappropriateness, low-confidence, or duplication. Subgroup representation (e.g., for FairFace categories) is balanced such that each key group composes at least 5% of its respective capability subset.\n\n---\n\n**Methodology: Data Curation and Infrastructure**  \nWe introduce a unified, **capability-driven data infrastructure** which defines and distinctly aligns three foundational generative capabilities:\n\n1. **Text-image grounding**: Aligns textual descriptions with semantically matched images. Annotation schemas strictly require text-image semantic matches, excluding knowledge/entity or editing metadata. Captioning combines automatic models (ensemble of BLIP, CLIP, and domain-specific models) with expert human annotators. Caption batches undergo double-blind review in >5% of cases, maintaining inter-annotator agreement (Cohen\u2019s \u03ba > 0.89).\n\n2. **Inter-image transformation**: Structures supervision for performing and recognizing image edits, based on before/after image pairs labeled with explicit, human-verifiable transformation tags (such as \"sky color change: blue\u2192black\"). Source data include benchmarks (e.g., CelebA-HQ Edits), algorithmic (CycleGAN/Ops) augmentations, and curated user edits. Edit saliency is empirically verified using ResNet-50 classifiers (ROC-AUC 94%) and random human audits where \u226590% of sampled pairs are recognized as intended edits.\n\n3. **Image-knowledge association**: Connects images with structured knowledge entities (e.g., cityscapes with Wikidata IDs like \"Bruges Belfry (Q2364)\"). Assignments are made with entity-linking tools (BLINK, TagMe), followed by adjudication from five human annotators in ambiguous or low-certainty scenarios (majority vote). Annotation explicitly restricts supervision to unambiguous entity codes, prohibiting open-ended text or edit signals.\n\nSeparation between capabilities is enforced using non-overlapping annotation fields, intentional avoidance of image duplication across tasks (unless explicitly required for benchmarking), and double-blind annotation on ambiguous cases. QA workflows and summarizing statistics are presented in Table 2 and Table A (above).\n\n**Figure 1 (Summary):** The illustration (not shown) schematically presents (a) the three data engines (one per capability), (b) the flow of data through filtering and QA, and (c) the curriculum scheduler module that manages training order based on the dependency graph. Example workflow: \u201ctext: a red fire truck\u201d paired with an image by the grounding engine; \u201coriginal image: cityscape \u2192 edited: nighttime cityscape\u201d sent to the transformation engine; \u201cphoto of a bell tower \u2192 Q2364\u201d processed by the association engine.\n\n---\n\n**Curriculum Scheduling and Task Dependency Construction:**  \nThe **curriculum scheduler** organizes model training order by leveraging empirically identified dependencies between capabilities:\n\n- To empirically build the capability dependency graph, we hold out a target capability (e.g., entity retrieval) during initial training, train on other foundational tasks (text-image or editing), and then measure downstream performance when the held-out task is included versus excluded.\n- Saliency of edit supervision is determined by both classifier-based scoring (as above) and confirmation by at least two independent annotators.\n- Metrics tracked: zero-shot retrieval accuracy, Fr\u00e9chet Inception Distance (FID; 50k sample protocol), edit classification accuracy, entity-level micro-precision.\n- Validation splits are constructed as stratified, non-overlapping samples: 10M text-image, 10M edit, and 2M entity examples, with subgroup-proportion checks.\n- Transfer is quantified: e.g., text-image grounding improves editing accuracy by an absolute 3.5% (from 59.6% to 63.1%), as established via paired ablations. Curriculum search uses a greedy procedure, increasing focus on underperforming dependencies if validation error increases.\n\n---\n\n**Bias Mitigation Protocols and Empirical Outcomes:**  \nBias mitigation is structured in three phases:\n\n1. **Subgroup Definition and Representation:** Following FairFace protocols (for race/ethnicity and gender) and Balanced ImageNet, every batch is monitored for subgroup representation: no key group may fall below five percent of its capability or the set median.\n\n2. **Thresholding, Rebalancing, and Synthetic Augmentation:** Batches falling below coverage are upsampled; >2 SD deviation triggers GAN-based synthetic augmentation (StyleGAN2). Attribute swapping and compositional blending are used for further diversification.\n   \n   - For example, underrepresented ethnicities in grounding and editing are increased from an average prevalence of 2.9% \u2192 7.1% via upsampling and GAN augmentation.\n\n3. **Audit and Reporting:** We sample 10,000 items per subgroup for bias auditing (classifier F1, coverage entropy, and accuracy). After interventions, underrepresented group accuracy increases from 79.2% (\u00b10.8%) to 87.1% (\u00b10.6%), with F1 gains up to 8 points. Mean class entropy increases from 0.65 to 0.79, and unique entity ratios improve by 11%. Specific results by subgroup and intervention type are tabulated in Table 3.\n\n---\n\n**Annotation Quality Assurance Protocols:**\n- Annotators must pass a language reasoning exam (\u226598%, 30 Q) and demonstrate \u226595% accuracy on a 50-item gold-standard benchmark specific to their annotation engine.\n- Ongoing calibration and regular confusion item training are provided, with >5% monthly spot-checks performed double-blind. The mean annotator error rate is 6.7%, largely due to lexical ambiguity; subgroup and task-level confusion rates are reported in Table 2.\n- Annotator-level bias is monitored by cross-comparing decisions on sensitive classes, and where systematic drift is detected, items are re-adjudicated by a different pool.\n\n---\n\n**Experimental Design and Empirical Evaluation:**  \nWe conduct comprehensive ablations:\n\n- **Curriculum Scheduling:** Disabling scheduling lowers CLIP retrieval accuracy from 80.6% to 77.1% on test sets (\u00b10.3% SD over 3 random splits).\n- **Editing Supervision:** Removing inter-image transformation data reduces edit classification from 63.1% to 55.8% (\u0394=7.3%, 95% CI [6.7, 7.8], p<0.01 paired t-test).\n- **Bias Protocols:** Omitting rebalancing/augmentation drops subgroup F1 from 82.5% to 74.5%, specifically disproportionately impacting rare ethnicities and fine-grained attributes (see Table 3).\n\n**Error Analysis and Qualitative Assessment Protocol:**  \nFor qualitative analysis, 1,000 samples per capability and subgroup are reviewed by independent annotators, who label primary failure types (semantic mismatch, entity mislabel, unattributable edits).\n\n- Frequent errors: rare compositional cues (e.g., \u201ctwo persons in traditional Sami dress\u201d), fine attribute swaps (\u201cgreen hat with blue logo\u201d), and obscure entity associations.\n- These errors occur most in entity association (<0.1% for rare entities), where micro-precision is up to 15% below common entities. Ratings are detailed in Table 4.\n\nQualitative examples (see Figure 2, described here): rows illustrate\u2014(1) complex prompt generalization (\u201ca red truck in heavy snow\u201d \u2192 \u201cremove snow\u201d); (2) composite edits (\u201ccity square, change day to night\u201d); (3) rare entity linking (correct/incorrect assignment to Wikidata IDs for minority architecture). Each column corresponds to a capability, showing before/after and error cases.\n\n---\n\n**Limitations and Caveats:**  \n1. **Long-tail entity coverage:** Largest models"}}